---
/**
 * /advisory/ — monthly AI advisory. THE FOURTH PAID THING ON THIS SITE, and the
 * first one that delivers no artifact.
 *
 * WHY IT HAS ITS OWN PAGE RATHER THAN A ROW ON /services/. Every other surface
 * here talks to a local owner who wants something built. This talks to somebody
 * at a manufacturer who has been asked what the company is doing about AI and
 * does not have an answer. Those are not the same reader, and a page serving
 * both asks each of them to sort themselves out of the other's copy — which the
 * one with a budget will not do. The ladder on /services/ stays about clients
 * who already have a build.
 *
 * IT PASSED THE FOURTH-PRODUCT TEST AND THE TEST WAS RUN RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
 * src/lib/pricing.js states it: a new published figure is allowed only for
 * something a visitor could buy without buying any of the others. Nobody here
 * buys a shelf tool, a site, or an assessment first. The argument is written out
 * above ADVISORY_MONTHLY_USD.
 *
 * WHY IT IS NOT THE "AI READINESS AUDIT" THIS PRACTICE REFUSES. src/data/home.ts
 * heads a column "What AI consulting usually ends with" and lists an AI
 * readiness audit inside it. D61 refuses selling one. The distinction is not
 * wording and it is not the roadmap — it is the three-month minimum. A roadmap
 * nobody is around to correct after thirty days IS that document. Somebody who
 * stays and is answerable for what they recommended is not, and the minimum is
 * the machinery that makes the difference real rather than claimed. Which is
 * also why the minimum renders on this page beside the figure and not in a term
 * sheet nobody opens.
 *
 * THE EMPLOYER IS NOT NAMED AND WILL NOT BE. D1 and D8 hold everywhere and this
 * page does not bend them. What it says is the SHAPE of the work — deciding
 * which of a long list of possible AI projects are real, which are vendor
 * theater, and what order the real ones go in. That is supportable from the
 * résumé, it claims no span of control, and the refusal to name is itself a
 * reason to trust the practice with somebody else's business. A draft of the
 * LinkedIn About once read "I run supply chain at a plant" and had to be pulled
 * back for claiming a span the title does not carry; the same line governs here.
 *
 * THE CONFLICT REFUSAL IS PART OF THE OFFER, NOT A DISCLAIMER AT THE BOTTOM.
 * Advising manufacturers while employed at one is only clean if the line is
 * drawn first and in public. It renders high on the page for that reason, and it
 * comes from src/data/services.ts so the same sentence reaches the retainer
 * agreement rather than being re-typed into it.
 *
 * NO PAY BUTTON, for the reason /ai-fit/ has none: this cannot be bought from a
 * page. It starts with a conversation, the retainer agreement is signed, and the
 * billing is set up inside that document — which already carries the
 * auto-renewal consent, the same-route cancellation and the annual reminder.
 *
 * THE META DESCRIPTION CARRIES NO FIGURE, for the reason /ai-fit/'s does not: a
 * monthly number without its minimum term is a smaller commitment than the one
 * being sold, and both together do not fit in what a search result shows.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { advisoryPrice, assessmentPrice } from '../data/services';
import { Compass, MessagesSquare, FileText, ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

const steps = [
  {
    icon: Compass,
    n: '01',
    t: 'The first thirty days produce the roadmap',
    b: 'Not a maturity score and not a readiness grade. A written list of the AI work worth doing in your business, in the order that pays, with what each one would cost and what it would return. It is the thing you put in front of a board, and it is yours whether or not you renew.',
  },
  {
    icon: MessagesSquare,
    n: '02',
    t: 'Then two or three working sessions a month',
    b: 'An hour each, and they are decisions rather than status updates. Which vendor is worth a pilot, whether the thing your team built should go to production, what to do about the department that started using something nobody approved.',
  },
  {
    icon: FileText,
    n: '03',
    t: 'And you can reach me in between',
    b: 'Answered inside 48 hours, any day of the week — weekends included, which is more than a firm keeping office hours will give you. Most of what this is actually for lives here: a place to send a proposal and get a straight answer on whether it is real, from somebody with no commission riding on it.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Service',
    '@id': `${origin}/advisory/#service`,
    name: 'Monthly AI advisory',
    serviceType: 'AI strategy advisory',
    provider: { '@id': `${origin}/#business` },
    description:
      'A monthly retainer for a company with no AI leadership: a roadmap in the first thirty days, working sessions twice or three times a month, and somebody answerable in between.',
  },
];
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="Monthly AI advisory"
  description="A monthly arrangement for a company that needs an AI direction and cannot hire someone to set one. You buy the decision, not a build."
  schema={schema}
>
  {/* ── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This page ran four bands on one
      ground with one rhythm, and the last two of them were byte-for-byte the
      same shell — `mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14 sm:pb-20` around a `panel p-8`,
      twice, with no top padding on either. Two arguments a reader is meant to
      weigh separately arrived as one column of identical cards. That is D113's
      complaint and it is also the complaint one level down from it, which is what
      /services/ answered with a narrower column rather than a new color.

      The ground now walks canvas, surface, canvas, deep; the column walks
      feature, content, popout, popout; the closing band is tightened under the
      one above it. Every neighbor differs in at least two heavy signals and the
      palette does none of the work — a hue is the lightest signal on a page,
      which is the reading in src/components/kit/industrial.js under WHY THREE
      AND NOT SEVEN and the reason Section.astro grew a `width` prop at all.

      THE DEEP GROUND IS SPENT ON WHAT THIS IS NOT. The header above says the
      three-month minimum is the whole difference between this and the AI
      readiness audit D61 refuses to sell, and that band is where the difference
      is argued. /ai-fit/ spends its own deep band on the section that turns a
      reader away for the same reason: the paragraph that costs the seller
      something is the one worth making a reader stop at.

      THE HERO IS LONG AND IT STAYS WHOLE. The conflict rule sits above the
      figure, the figure carries its qualifier, the exclusions sit beside the
      inclusions, and each of those placements has a comment below saying why. A
      band edge anywhere inside that sequence would put a term on the far side of
      a ground change from the number it bounds, which is the one failure mode of
      publishing a price at all. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <p class="mono-label">A roadmap · sessions twice a month · somebody answerable</p>
      <h1 class="statement mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        Somebody asked what you are doing about AI.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        You do not need another platform and you probably do not need a project yet. You need
        somebody who has made these decisions at a scale above yours to tell you which of your
        options is real.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        That is what this is. Nothing gets built and nothing gets licensed.
      </p>

      {/* THE REFUSAL SITS ABOVE THE FIGURE DELIBERATELY. A reader at a
          manufacturer works out the conflict question before they work out the
          price, and answering it after the number reads as a disclaimer rather
          than as a term. */}
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{advisoryPrice.conflictRule}</p>

      <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl">
        <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-2 p-6">
          <p class="font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{advisoryPrice.line}</p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{advisoryPrice.qualifier}</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-3xl gap-2">
        {advisoryPrice.includes.map((item) => (
          <li class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">— {item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {/* WHAT IS EXCLUDED RENDERS BESIDE WHAT IS INCLUDED, never a screen below
          it. A retainer whose edge is stated somewhere the buyer does not reach
          has no edge, and this is the one that decides whether a monthly
          arrangement quietly becomes an unpaid build. */}
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Not included:</span> {advisoryPrice.excludes}
      </p>

      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        {/* `btn` CARRIES THE SHAPE AND `btn-primary` ONLY THE PAINT, and this
            call to action shipped without the first of them — the same defect
            /quoted/, /upkeep/, /visibility/ and /rescue/ were found with, on the
            same day and by the same method, which is looking at the page.
            global.css:474 holds the padding, the inline-flex row and the corner
            radius; :495 adds the gradient and the glow on top. Alone, the second
            paints a hard-edged rectangle with the label pressed against its
            sides and the arrow wrapped onto a second line. No gate sees it:
            nothing here measures a control's geometry, and verify-bands.mjs
            skips the inside of `.btn-primary` by name. */}
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/?intent=consult">
          Start with a conversation <ArrowUpRight class="size-4" />
        </a>
        <a class="link-sweep text-sm" href="/ai-fit/">Or start with a paid read of the work →</a>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        {assessmentPrice.credit.line}
      </p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── What a month looks like — the one banded ground on this page ───────── */}
  <Section tone="surface">
    <p class="mono-label">What a month looks like</p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {steps.map((s) => (
        <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <s.icon class="size-5 text-accent" />
            <span class="mono-label">{s.n}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{s.t}</p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{s.b}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── Why me — back to canvas, and the narrowest column on the page ──────── */}
  <Section width="popout">
    <div class="panel p-8">
      <p class="mono-label">Why me</p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        I do this work at scale for a manufacturer with plants across North America — shaping the
        path, the scope and the implementations. I am not able to name them, and you would not want
        an advisor who named their other clients anyway.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        What I can tell you is what the job actually is. Deciding which of a long list of possible
        AI projects are real, which are vendor theater, and what order the real ones go in. That is
        the same decision you are facing, with a larger number in front of it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {/* FOUR, NOT THREE AND NOT SEVEN — the operator's call, 2026-09-13, when
            every short version of the career on this site turned out to be
            dropping a domain. This one named three and left out both insurance
            and the plant. The plant is back because it is the current and
            largest operation and the one a company buying advice weighs most;
            the list stays short because this page argues judgment rather than
            breadth, and /ai-fit/ is where the full run belongs. Anything shorter
            than this understates him, which is the rule everywhere on the
            site. */}
        Before any of that I ran the operations the software is supposed to improve — a restaurant
        floor at a casino, a bank's lending division, a clinical practice's front office, and now the
        planning floor of the appliance plant in town. I have been
        the person whose week gets worse when somebody buys the wrong system.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── What this is not — the deep ground, spent here ──────────────────────
      Same column as the band above it, deliberately: this is still addressed to
      the reader who has just finished that one, and moving the measure under
      them would read as a new subject rather than as the last word on this one.
      The ground and the rhythm are what separate them, which is the trade the
      hero comment sets out. */}
  <Section tone="deep" width="popout" density="tight">
    <div class="panel p-8">
      <p class="mono-label">What this is not</p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        It is not an AI readiness audit. That ends in a document, and a document nobody is around to
        correct is worth what you paid to file it. The {advisoryPrice.minimumMonths}-month minimum is
        the difference: I am still here when the first recommendation meets reality, and I am
        answerable for it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        It is also not a build. Where the roadmap says something is worth doing, that gets scoped and
        quoted on its own — by me or by anybody else you like. The roadmap is written so another
        developer can work from it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
